Welcome back to The Rest is History.

488
00:34:59,270 --> 00:35:01,650
The good news is that Tom has dried his tears.

489
00:35:01,850 --> 00:35:07,650
The bad news is that Louis Capet is facing an appointment with Madame Guillotine.

490
00:35:08,050 --> 00:35:16,050
So Tom, we ended with Malzaheb's sobbing, announced the verdict to Louis.

491
00:35:16,430 --> 00:35:23,010
And then the convention themselves sent a delegation to Louis, don't they, to sort of formally read the verdict to him.

492
00:35:23,010 --> 00:35:25,730
So that's three days after the trial.

493
00:35:26,050 --> 00:35:32,570
And he remarkably still is very composed and indeed serene when he receives them.

494
00:35:32,890 --> 00:35:33,390
Yeah.

495
00:35:33,690 --> 00:35:43,030
Everyone comments on this, that almost from the moment he's brought to trial, he does maintain this kind of incredible display of sans-froid, I guess is the only word for it.

496
00:35:45,150 --> 00:35:52,790
So the delegation from the convention come to see him on the afternoon of the 20th of January.

497
00:35:52,790 --> 00:35:54,050
So that's a Sunday.

498
00:35:54,190 --> 00:35:57,210
And they arrive early afternoon at two o'clock.

499
00:35:57,630 --> 00:36:05,650
And the delegation includes Jacques Hébert, who is the guy who's always writing abusive pamphlets and so on.

500
00:36:06,310 --> 00:36:09,150
Coming up with the kind of Trumpian nicknames for his enemies.

501
00:36:09,350 --> 00:36:09,670
Yeah.

502
00:36:09,950 --> 00:36:12,750
And the Minister of Justice, who is a man called Joseph Garin.

503
00:36:13,290 --> 00:36:18,230
And they come into Louis' cell and they read out the sentence of the court to him.

504
00:36:19,370 --> 00:36:22,770
And Louis has prepared a list written.

505
00:36:22,770 --> 00:36:24,190
A written list of three requests.

506
00:36:24,850 --> 00:36:31,630
So the first of these, he asks a delay of three days to be able to prepare myself to appear in the presence of God.

507
00:36:32,190 --> 00:36:33,050
This is rejected.

508
00:36:33,290 --> 00:36:35,670
He's told he is going to be executed in the morning.

509
00:36:35,770 --> 00:36:37,130
So on the 21st of January.

510
00:36:37,270 --> 00:36:39,630
Then he requests freedom for his family.

511
00:36:39,830 --> 00:36:50,310
And you said how when he was with them for the four months of his imprisonment before the trial start, he had been living like a kind of middle class family under COVID.

512
00:36:50,510 --> 00:36:50,950
Yeah.

513
00:36:50,950 --> 00:36:53,610
You know, a garden, homeschooling, all that kind of thing.

514
00:36:54,470 --> 00:36:59,850
So I guess he's had a kind of last experience as the family man that I think he always was at heart.

515
00:37:00,750 --> 00:37:01,730
So he's had that.

516
00:37:01,830 --> 00:37:05,110
But now he wants to make sure that they will be set free.

517
00:37:05,990 --> 00:37:07,630
They will not be set free.

518
00:37:07,790 --> 00:37:09,510
So this request too will be rejected.

519
00:37:10,010 --> 00:37:11,690
But he is told by Garin.

520
00:37:11,770 --> 00:37:14,290
I mean, Garin doesn't dash his hopes at that point.

521
00:37:14,390 --> 00:37:18,890
So he says that the nation always magnanimous, always just will consider your family's fate.

522
00:37:19,090 --> 00:37:20,530
So Louis can go to the guillotine.

523
00:37:21,190 --> 00:37:26,050
Hoping at least that he's secured the possibility of liberty for his family.

524
00:37:26,270 --> 00:37:26,470
Yeah.

525
00:37:26,610 --> 00:37:33,150
One kind of sub request to that though is granted, which is that Louis will be allowed to see his family for one last time.

526
00:37:33,290 --> 00:37:35,210
So as we said, they've been separated.

527
00:37:35,970 --> 00:37:38,170
And then his third request is also granted.

528
00:37:38,230 --> 00:37:48,350
And this is to hear confession from a priest who has maintained his independence from the kind of the Republican church that's been set up.

529
00:37:48,350 --> 00:37:50,110
So a non-juring priest, as they're called.

530
00:37:50,110 --> 00:37:52,430
One who hasn't sworn the oath of loyalty to the Republic.

531
00:37:53,250 --> 00:38:00,810
And so Louis has had a recommendation from his sister, Madame Elizabeth, who is imprisoned in the temple with him.

532
00:38:01,230 --> 00:38:03,270
And this amazingly is an Irishman.

533
00:38:03,370 --> 00:38:11,130
It's a guy called Henry Essex Edgeworth, who is the son of an Irish convert and is related to Maria Edgeworth.

534
00:38:11,350 --> 00:38:12,310
The novelist. Yeah.

535
00:38:12,430 --> 00:38:12,650
Yeah.

536
00:38:12,750 --> 00:38:19,750
So he'd been the son of a Church of Ireland priest who had converted to Catholicism when Henry was four.

537
00:38:19,750 --> 00:38:20,690
And they'd moved to France.

538
00:38:20,950 --> 00:38:24,270
And Edgeworth had been brought up by the Jesuits in Toulouse.

539
00:38:24,410 --> 00:38:25,310
He'd become a priest.

540
00:38:25,410 --> 00:38:27,150
He'd become l'abbé de Firmand.

541
00:38:27,490 --> 00:38:31,670
And he had become Madame Elizabeth's confessor in 1791.

542
00:38:32,750 --> 00:38:37,910
And he's very respected, even by the kind of the sans-culottes, by the revolutionary leaders.

543
00:38:38,130 --> 00:38:38,750
He's admired.

544
00:38:39,570 --> 00:38:44,410
And so he is allowed access to the king or the ex-king, I should say.

545
00:38:44,930 --> 00:38:48,070
And he arrives at the temple that evening at seven o'clock.

546
00:38:48,250 --> 00:38:49,730
And from this point on, he will stay there.

547
00:38:49,730 --> 00:38:51,670
He will stay with the king until the very point of his execution.

548
00:38:52,430 --> 00:38:58,990
So an hour after the abbé de Firmand has arrived, Louis' family are brought into his presence.

549
00:38:59,190 --> 00:39:06,390
So that's Marie-Antoinette, the dauphin, his son, his daughter, Madame Royale, and his sister, Madame Elizabeth.

550
00:39:07,350 --> 00:39:09,210
And they're taken into the cell.

551
00:39:09,430 --> 00:39:12,770
And although the guards keep watch, they can't hear what is said.

552
00:39:12,930 --> 00:39:16,910
And the best account we have of this comes from the man who's been serving Louis as his valet.

553
00:39:16,910 --> 00:39:18,690
He's a man called Jean-Baptiste Cléry.

554
00:39:19,730 --> 00:39:25,690
And he had been appointed to the role of valet by the municipality of Paris.

555
00:39:26,270 --> 00:39:27,490
He's a Republican.

556
00:39:27,590 --> 00:39:28,450
He's a revolutionary.

557
00:39:28,830 --> 00:39:34,330
But over the course of his term of service, he has basically been turned.

558
00:39:34,610 --> 00:39:42,390
You know, he comes to admire and respect Louis for his serene behavior, for his sang-froid, for his stiff upper lip.

559
00:39:43,130 --> 00:39:49,310
And in due course, he comes to write a very favorable account of Louis' last months and days.

560
00:39:49,730 --> 00:39:49,930
And hours.

561
00:39:50,310 --> 00:39:53,810
And he says of this meeting, this last meeting between Louis and his family,

562
00:39:53,930 --> 00:39:55,430
it was impossible to hear anything.

563
00:39:55,530 --> 00:39:59,810
One only saw that after each phrase of the king, the sobs of the princesses redoubled.

564
00:39:59,870 --> 00:40:01,790
And that then the king began again to speak.

565
00:40:02,050 --> 00:40:07,290
It was easy to judge from their movements that he himself had told them of his condemnation.

566
00:40:07,770 --> 00:40:09,410
And they're together for two hours.

567
00:40:09,610 --> 00:40:11,850
And they can't bear to be parted.

568
00:40:11,890 --> 00:40:16,450
And in the end, it's Louis himself who says, look, don't worry.

569
00:40:16,590 --> 00:40:19,050
You can see me again tomorrow morning.

570
00:40:19,050 --> 00:40:20,610
Before I go to the guillotine.

571
00:40:20,770 --> 00:40:21,730
And this is a lie.

572
00:40:21,950 --> 00:40:25,890
But it's designed to get them out of the cell in a mood of hope.

573
00:40:26,330 --> 00:40:34,950
But as the family are being ushered out, his daughter, the princess royal, bursts into floods of tears, hugs her father, collapses in a dead faint.

574
00:40:35,210 --> 00:40:38,190
And the family will gather around, bring her back.

575
00:40:38,790 --> 00:40:42,110
And this is the last time that they spend time together.

576
00:40:42,270 --> 00:40:43,390
And she's a teenager, isn't she?

577
00:40:43,490 --> 00:40:44,570
She's what, 15 or so?

578
00:40:44,730 --> 00:40:45,110
Yeah.

579
00:40:45,510 --> 00:40:46,810
So that's that.

580
00:40:46,890 --> 00:40:48,650
That's the last time that they are together.

581
00:40:48,650 --> 00:40:53,670
Meanwhile, the king is not the only person who has been hanging out with a priest.

582
00:40:54,110 --> 00:40:56,830
Because someone else has been receiving absolution.

583
00:40:57,110 --> 00:41:00,650
And this is someone that we mentioned in our last series.

584
00:41:00,850 --> 00:41:06,430
It's the man who will be operating the guillotine and executing the king the next morning.

585
00:41:06,610 --> 00:41:12,450
And this is Henri Samson, the man who had been the royal executioner.

586
00:41:12,470 --> 00:41:16,830
And who is, I guess, a kind of living link between the Ancien Régime

587
00:41:16,830 --> 00:41:21,790
and the new world that the revolution has ushered in.

588
00:41:21,870 --> 00:41:26,990
Because he's the sixth generation, I think, of Samson's who've been executioners, isn't he?

589
00:41:27,250 --> 00:41:27,810
Yes.

590
00:41:27,850 --> 00:41:34,250
And we did an episode on how all the kind of the weirdness surrounding executions

591
00:41:34,250 --> 00:41:41,590
and indeed the role of the executioner had been systematically got rid of by the Republican legislators.

592
00:41:42,010 --> 00:41:46,710
So Samson, who had been the sinister figure in scarlet robes, who could just walk through marketplace,

593
00:41:46,830 --> 00:41:50,650
and grab whatever he wanted, and wasn't allowed to go to theatres and things.

594
00:41:50,810 --> 00:41:52,630
He's now a state functionary.

595
00:41:52,710 --> 00:41:58,010
And the emphasis is on the rational, the mechanical, the everyday.

596
00:41:58,370 --> 00:42:06,810
And so it's been decided that the king will be executed like any other criminal now in France by the guillotine.

597
00:42:06,990 --> 00:42:12,810
And because they want a stage suitable for the execution of a former king,

598
00:42:12,950 --> 00:42:16,650
they fix on the largest open square in the whole of Paris.

599
00:42:16,650 --> 00:42:20,050
Which is what today is the Place de la Concorde,

600
00:42:20,170 --> 00:42:23,790
but had originally been the Place Louis XV.

601
00:42:25,030 --> 00:42:31,570
So there'd been a huge statue of Louis XV on a pedestal that had been removed back in August 1792.

602
00:42:31,770 --> 00:42:33,150
So the pedestal is now empty.

603
00:42:33,290 --> 00:42:36,550
And actually in due course, a large statue of liberty will be set up on it.

604
00:42:36,890 --> 00:42:39,270
It's also next to the Tuileries.

605
00:42:39,410 --> 00:42:42,150
So there's a kind of symbolic resonance there as well.

606
00:42:42,450 --> 00:42:44,750
Because the Tuileries is seen as, you know,

607
00:42:44,770 --> 00:42:46,530
this place where the blood of citizens,

608
00:42:46,650 --> 00:42:49,270
the blood of citizens have been spilled on the orders of the tyrant.

609
00:42:49,430 --> 00:42:52,850
So it's fitting that he be put to death within eyesight of it.

610
00:42:53,490 --> 00:42:57,830
And the Place Louis XV has been renamed the Place de la Révolution.

611
00:42:58,490 --> 00:43:01,090
Symbolically, it works on pretty much every level.

612
00:43:01,330 --> 00:43:03,390
And so the guillotine is brought there.

613
00:43:03,570 --> 00:43:04,950
It's set up in the middle of it.

614
00:43:05,190 --> 00:43:09,870
And Samson, you know, he checks the hinges.

615
00:43:09,930 --> 00:43:14,910
He gets out the oil, makes sure the blade is properly weighted, sharpens it.

616
00:43:14,910 --> 00:43:21,590
And the whole point is the death is something kind of Republican, something progressive.

617
00:43:22,070 --> 00:43:23,250
Clean and modern.

618
00:43:23,390 --> 00:43:24,330
Clean and modern.

619
00:43:24,790 --> 00:43:30,130
So not appropriate to the dispatch, not just of a king, but of monarchy itself.

620
00:43:30,550 --> 00:43:30,670
Yeah.

621
00:43:30,830 --> 00:43:36,850
So the day dawns and massive preparations have been made.

622
00:43:37,070 --> 00:43:44,370
So armed men, soldiers, members of the National Guard have been ordered to line the entire route

623
00:43:44,370 --> 00:43:50,590
from the temple to the Place de la Révolution to make sure that the crowds are kept back.

624
00:43:50,710 --> 00:43:56,630
And more importantly, that royalists can't kind of try and make an attempt to rescue the king or anything like that.

625
00:43:56,850 --> 00:44:00,090
Again, this is very Charles I in Whitehall, isn't it?

626
00:44:00,110 --> 00:44:01,850
Absolutely the same scenario.

627
00:44:02,410 --> 00:44:07,030
One thing that isn't like Charles I, though, where members of the general public were allowed to watch the execution.

628
00:44:07,190 --> 00:44:10,070
In the Place de la Révolution, it's only soldiers.

629
00:44:10,270 --> 00:44:13,370
So there are only armed men, about 80,000 of them.

630
00:44:13,910 --> 00:44:14,350
Right.

631
00:44:14,370 --> 00:44:15,930
There are 84 pieces of artillery.

632
00:44:16,270 --> 00:44:20,310
So the people there, they're armed and they're complete revolutionaries.

633
00:44:20,330 --> 00:44:21,030
They're complete Republicans.

634
00:44:21,330 --> 00:44:22,150
There's no doubt about that.

635
00:44:22,290 --> 00:44:24,570
If the guillotine doesn't work, they can turn their cannons on him.

636
00:44:25,150 --> 00:44:26,590
Exactly. Blast him to pieces.

637
00:44:27,010 --> 00:44:37,710
So orders are issued that anyone who cries out for the king to be pardoned or who gives any manifestation of royalist sympathy at all will be arrested and imprisoned.

638
00:44:38,890 --> 00:44:43,810
Because the delegates to the convention, they're worried about the natural compassion.

639
00:44:46,410 --> 00:44:50,190
They're worried that women are more prone to displays of sympathy than men.

640
00:44:50,270 --> 00:44:53,930
A decree is ordered that all women should stay in their homes.

641
00:44:54,050 --> 00:44:56,810
They're not allowed out to watch the display and execution.

642
00:44:57,170 --> 00:44:59,370
All houses are ordered to be shuttered.

643
00:44:59,390 --> 00:45:03,950
So again, people can't lean out and kind of watch the passing display.

644
00:45:05,110 --> 00:45:13,790
And the mayor of Paris has a very stentorian notice plastered across the walls of the city so that everyone can read it.

645
00:45:13,790 --> 00:45:20,610
The sword of the law, this notice declares, will strike the greatest and the guiltiest of conspirators.

646
00:45:20,770 --> 00:45:26,630
You have, citizens of Paris, during the course of this long trial, maintained the calm that is suitable for free men.

647
00:45:26,830 --> 00:45:30,510
You will know how to preserve it at the instant of the execution of the tyrant.

648
00:45:30,630 --> 00:45:36,130
You will prove by the decorum of your behavior that an act of justice in no way resembles vengeance.

649
00:45:36,430 --> 00:45:42,470
That day will be for kings and for peoples at once a memorable example of the just punishment of despots

650
00:45:42,470 --> 00:45:47,710
and of the mournful dignity that a sovereign people must keep in the exercise of its power.

651
00:45:47,930 --> 00:45:51,790
And the thing is, people in the revolution just cannot stop talking like that.

652
00:45:51,890 --> 00:45:53,890
That's true. Yeah.

653
00:45:53,970 --> 00:46:01,730
All speeches, all public proclamations, they all sound like they've been lifted from, you know, a school book of Latin prose.

654
00:46:02,190 --> 00:46:03,930
Yeah, they do, don't they? Well, they're doing it deliberately.

655
00:46:04,150 --> 00:46:06,470
That's how they think a revolutionary should write.

656
00:46:06,690 --> 00:46:12,350
So early hours of the morning, the streets of Paris are sounding to the rumble of the artillery being taken.

657
00:46:12,350 --> 00:46:17,710
Into the Place de la Révolution, you hear the clopping of hooves of the cavalry riding to take up place.

658
00:46:17,850 --> 00:46:19,390
The tread of soldiers taking up their positions.

659
00:46:20,070 --> 00:46:27,030
5 a.m., Samson, the executioner, is wakened by his assistant so that he can go again, you know, check all the mechanics and everything.

660
00:46:27,190 --> 00:46:28,270
He doesn't want it to go wrong.

661
00:46:28,690 --> 00:46:35,450
And at the same time in the temple, 5 o'clock, Louis is wakened by Clary, his valet, who shaves him.

662
00:46:35,910 --> 00:46:40,750
6 a.m., Louis' last confession with the Abbé de Firmont, his confessor.

663
00:46:40,750 --> 00:46:43,750
He has not eaten the night before.

664
00:46:43,790 --> 00:46:47,610
He doesn't eat now because the last rites are accompanied by fasting.

665
00:46:47,850 --> 00:46:50,410
7 a.m., he is dressed by Clary.

666
00:46:50,870 --> 00:46:56,330
He wears, we're told, a fresh shirt, a white waistcoat, grey trousers, a light jacket.

667
00:46:56,610 --> 00:46:59,670
And Louis takes off his wedding ring to be given to Marie Antoinette.

668
00:47:00,350 --> 00:47:03,890
And he has a royal seal, which he's taken from his watch.

669
00:47:03,990 --> 00:47:05,650
So that hadn't been confiscated.

670
00:47:05,710 --> 00:47:07,430
And this is to be given to his son.

671
00:47:08,370 --> 00:47:10,730
And his son, of course, before the morn.

672
00:47:10,750 --> 00:47:15,530
The morning is out, will become, if you are a royalist, Louis XVII.

673
00:47:16,010 --> 00:47:19,210
7.45, a delegation from the city of Paris arrives.

674
00:47:19,510 --> 00:47:21,350
They are accompanied by 14 soldiers.

675
00:47:21,850 --> 00:47:24,750
They stand to attention in two rows outside Louis' cell.

676
00:47:25,050 --> 00:47:31,330
Louis asks for permission for Clary to cut his hair at the nape of his neck

677
00:47:31,330 --> 00:47:35,570
because he doesn't want the humiliation of having Samson, the executioner, do it.

678
00:47:35,650 --> 00:47:40,130
Which is something, of course, that Samson has done many, many times over the course of his career.

679
00:47:40,130 --> 00:47:41,570
But this is denied him.

680
00:47:41,630 --> 00:47:42,690
He is a common criminal.

681
00:47:42,850 --> 00:47:44,470
He is going to be treated like a common criminal.

682
00:47:44,650 --> 00:47:45,590
Louis then kneels down.

683
00:47:45,750 --> 00:47:46,950
He's blessed by the Abbe.

684
00:47:47,730 --> 00:47:48,970
They then stand around.

685
00:47:49,130 --> 00:47:49,830
It's a bit of embarrassment.

686
00:47:50,170 --> 00:47:51,150
Nobody's quite sure what to do.

687
00:47:51,290 --> 00:47:52,090
So Louis then says,

688
00:47:52,130 --> 00:47:52,270
Pardon.

689
00:47:53,170 --> 00:47:53,850
Let's head off.

690
00:47:53,930 --> 00:47:54,270
Let's go.

691
00:47:54,530 --> 00:47:55,310
They go outside.

692
00:47:55,630 --> 00:47:56,630
It's very cold.

693
00:47:56,830 --> 00:47:57,930
There's a fog everywhere.

694
00:47:58,070 --> 00:48:00,450
So hard to see through the gloom.

695
00:48:00,890 --> 00:48:05,330
Out in the streets, piles of snow so that people can get by along the road.

696
00:48:05,510 --> 00:48:08,410
I said that Louis is to be treated like a common criminal.

697
00:48:08,470 --> 00:48:09,770
There is one exception to this.

698
00:48:10,130 --> 00:48:11,650
And that is that he is given a coach.

699
00:48:11,830 --> 00:48:14,350
So he's not put in the tumbril, in the open cart.

700
00:48:14,630 --> 00:48:16,750
And the reason for that is basically security.

701
00:48:17,010 --> 00:48:19,190
If he's in the coach, it's much easier to guard him.

702
00:48:19,350 --> 00:48:22,770
And the coach has been provided by Garab, the Minister of Justice.

703
00:48:23,090 --> 00:48:26,970
It's a great bottle green thing, although not as large as the coach.

704
00:48:27,450 --> 00:48:28,990
Not the Varen super coach.

705
00:48:29,330 --> 00:48:32,890
The Varen wasp-like coach that they take into Varen.

706
00:48:33,090 --> 00:48:34,430
So Louis gets into the coach.

707
00:48:34,650 --> 00:48:40,050
There are two members of the security services, I suppose you could say, sitting on either side.

708
00:48:40,130 --> 00:48:41,610
The Abbe sits at the front.

709
00:48:41,670 --> 00:48:47,490
And the whole way there, Louis is murmuring the prayer of the dying over and over to himself.

710
00:48:47,590 --> 00:48:49,350
Otherwise, he doesn't say anything.

711
00:48:49,890 --> 00:48:57,670
And as they set off, there are 100 mounted police behind it, 100 mounted members of the National Guard.

712
00:48:57,930 --> 00:49:09,290
So the rumbling of the wheels, the clopping of the horses, generally silence from the watching crowds who are massed behind the lines of the guards along the route.

713
00:49:09,290 --> 00:49:11,150
They set off at 8 o'clock.

714
00:49:11,450 --> 00:49:15,070
And it takes them two hours to get there.

715
00:49:15,690 --> 00:49:19,750
And in the course of those two hours, there are two royalist attempts to reach Louis.

716
00:49:19,930 --> 00:49:22,310
They're both easily subdued, easily stopped.

717
00:49:22,550 --> 00:49:26,690
And at 10 o'clock, the carriage arrives in the Place de la Révolution.

718
00:49:27,710 --> 00:49:30,990
And it stands still for five minutes.

719
00:49:31,570 --> 00:49:36,110
And then Samson's assistant steps up to it, opens up the door.

720
00:49:36,210 --> 00:49:37,890
The steps are pulled down.

721
00:49:38,390 --> 00:49:38,910
And...

722
00:49:39,830 --> 00:49:44,770
Louis is seized by Samson and his assistants.

723
00:49:46,210 --> 00:49:51,150
When they touch him, Louis loses his sense of composure.

724
00:49:51,350 --> 00:49:54,650
Because we've talked about that sense that the king's body is inviolate.

725
00:49:54,850 --> 00:49:57,190
How dare people manhandle him?

726
00:49:57,410 --> 00:50:00,470
But then he catches the eye of his confessor, of the Abbe.

727
00:50:00,650 --> 00:50:03,130
And the Abbe kind of says, you know, don't do this.

728
00:50:03,190 --> 00:50:05,470
This is, you don't need to do this.

729
00:50:05,850 --> 00:50:09,270
And clearly, they've talked about the passion of the King.

730
00:50:09,270 --> 00:50:09,610
The passion of Christ.

731
00:50:09,970 --> 00:50:15,090
And essentially, Louis is thinking, well, you know, his saviour suffered worse.

732
00:50:15,430 --> 00:50:20,630
So he accepts to be handled by the executioners.

733
00:50:21,310 --> 00:50:24,110
So he then, he takes off his coat.

734
00:50:24,330 --> 00:50:28,170
And he pulls down his shirt so that his neck is open.

735
00:50:28,930 --> 00:50:31,250
His wrists are bound behind his back.

736
00:50:31,610 --> 00:50:33,050
His wig is taken off.

737
00:50:33,170 --> 00:50:36,090
And then Samson comes with the shears, with the clippers.

738
00:50:36,190 --> 00:50:38,090
And his hair is cut.

739
00:50:38,090 --> 00:50:40,090
So that the nape of his neck is exposed.

740
00:50:40,470 --> 00:50:41,950
He climbs up to the guillotine.

741
00:50:41,990 --> 00:50:45,490
He has to lean on the Abbe because he's got his hands behind his back.

742
00:50:45,550 --> 00:50:46,510
So a bit unstable.

743
00:50:46,990 --> 00:50:50,090
And when he gets onto the scaffold, he tries to address the crowd.

744
00:50:50,230 --> 00:50:52,190
And he seems to have uttered a few sentences.

745
00:50:52,770 --> 00:50:55,730
So according to various accounts, he says,

746
00:50:55,830 --> 00:50:58,230
People, I die innocent of the crimes that they ascribe to me.

747
00:50:58,310 --> 00:50:59,910
I forgive the authors of my death.

748
00:50:59,970 --> 00:51:04,030
And I pray to God that the blood you are about to spill will never fall back upon France.

749
00:51:04,310 --> 00:51:07,330
And at this point, there is a great drum roll.

750
00:51:07,330 --> 00:51:13,050
The captain of the guard has been prepared to do this should Louis try to make a public speech.

751
00:51:13,290 --> 00:51:15,370
And so Louis' voice is drowned out.

752
00:51:15,550 --> 00:51:19,250
As the drums are rolling, Samson's assistants get hold of Louis.

753
00:51:19,470 --> 00:51:21,350
They strap him down onto the board.

754
00:51:21,430 --> 00:51:26,310
And they push him out so that his head is protruding, ready for the blade to cut through the neck.

755
00:51:26,490 --> 00:51:28,530
It's Samson himself who pulls on the cord.

756
00:51:28,930 --> 00:51:32,590
The blade hisses down, cuts straight through Louis' neck.

757
00:51:32,710 --> 00:51:34,610
And the head drops into the basket.

758
00:51:34,810 --> 00:51:36,710
And it is 10.22.

759
00:51:37,730 --> 00:51:41,470
Samson then does what he'd do with any beheaded criminal.

760
00:51:41,630 --> 00:51:44,910
He picks up the head and he shows it to the crowd.

761
00:51:45,110 --> 00:51:46,790
And the people roar back in answer.

762
00:51:46,910 --> 00:51:47,530
Vive la nation.

763
00:51:47,890 --> 00:51:48,990
Vive la republique.

764
00:51:50,170 --> 00:51:55,590
There is then the sound of gunfire, of artillery to mark the moment.

765
00:51:55,930 --> 00:51:59,610
And in the distant temple, Marie-Antoinette hears it.

766
00:51:59,690 --> 00:52:01,430
She knows what it signals.

767
00:52:01,710 --> 00:52:06,690
And she drops to her knees before her son, who a few minutes before had been killed.

768
00:52:06,710 --> 00:52:13,610
And now, in the opinion of Marie-Antoinette and all royalists in France, is Louis XVII.

769
00:52:14,030 --> 00:52:15,370
Crikey, what a moment.

770
00:52:15,630 --> 00:52:16,690
So you mentioned the crowd.

771
00:52:16,950 --> 00:52:18,870
The crowd, of course, are mainly troops.

772
00:52:19,270 --> 00:52:24,150
Because the ordinary people of Paris have been kept away from this moment.

773
00:52:24,590 --> 00:52:30,730
But within a couple of hours of this, am I not right in thinking that the troops have moved away?

774
00:52:30,910 --> 00:52:32,170
People come into the streets.

775
00:52:32,250 --> 00:52:34,050
It's a little bit like on a public holiday.

776
00:52:34,170 --> 00:52:35,170
People are strolling around.

777
00:52:35,170 --> 00:52:39,250
And, you know, I know it's January, so it's not exactly as though they're enjoying the sunshine.

778
00:52:39,470 --> 00:52:43,070
But people are acting as though it's just an ordinary Sunday or something.

779
00:52:43,550 --> 00:52:46,290
Sort of taking the air as though nothing has happened.

780
00:52:46,630 --> 00:52:54,630
So Marat, a couple of days later, he describes the public mood on that day in the hours that follow Louis' execution.

781
00:52:54,910 --> 00:52:57,970
And he says that the people seemed animated with a serene joy.

782
00:52:58,430 --> 00:53:02,270
One might have said that they had just attended a religious festival.

783
00:53:02,610 --> 00:53:05,150
And he might well have put it like that.

784
00:53:05,150 --> 00:53:11,370
Because essentially, and Marat is very conscious of this, what has been staged is a kind of ritual of sacrifice.

785
00:53:12,450 --> 00:53:19,030
And it's a sacrifice that has enabled the Republic to be born in a way that it had not previously been born.

786
00:53:19,190 --> 00:53:23,270
So Marat, in the article he wrote on the 23rd, so two days after the execution, he goes on to say,

787
00:53:23,310 --> 00:53:24,990
The head of the tyrant has just fallen.

788
00:53:25,110 --> 00:53:28,310
The same blow has overthrown the foundations of monarchy among us.

789
00:53:28,350 --> 00:53:29,190
His life is no more.

790
00:53:29,290 --> 00:53:31,430
His body is now nothing but a corpse.

791
00:53:32,150 --> 00:53:34,270
At last, I believe in the Republic.

792
00:53:34,270 --> 00:53:36,290
It's amazing, the Marat of all people.

793
00:53:36,490 --> 00:53:42,710
This is what it has taken for him to believe that the Republic exists at last.

794
00:53:43,050 --> 00:53:44,750
And there's a massive paradox here.

795
00:53:44,890 --> 00:53:49,890
Because Louis did have to die as a king for the Republic to be born.

796
00:53:49,950 --> 00:53:53,970
Because otherwise the sacrifice wouldn't have had the quality that it does have.

797
00:53:54,730 --> 00:53:58,250
But the moment he's dead and the Republic has been given existence,

798
00:53:58,910 --> 00:54:02,890
then his body has to be treated as though he had been nothing.

799
00:54:02,950 --> 00:54:04,250
As though he's just an ordinary person.

800
00:54:04,270 --> 00:54:05,930
An ordinary citizen convicted of a crime.

801
00:54:06,550 --> 00:54:08,370
Nothing more, nothing less.

802
00:54:08,570 --> 00:54:11,450
No kind of special treatment in any way.

803
00:54:12,010 --> 00:54:17,730
So certainly Louis' body is not allowed to be buried in the royal catacombs or anything like that.

804
00:54:17,930 --> 00:54:22,270
There had been a request before the execution from royalists for him to be buried next to his father.

805
00:54:22,350 --> 00:54:23,230
That had been denied.

806
00:54:24,950 --> 00:54:27,870
But at the same time, no special kind of desecration.

807
00:54:28,050 --> 00:54:31,450
No humiliations perpetrated on the corpse.

808
00:54:31,450 --> 00:54:36,230
Instead, he has to be buried, the convention has decreed,

809
00:54:36,310 --> 00:54:40,570
in the ordinary place of burial for citizens in the section that includes where he will be executed.

810
00:54:40,810 --> 00:54:41,590
That's what they've said.

811
00:54:42,210 --> 00:54:44,930
Just as though he's any other condemned criminal.

812
00:54:45,330 --> 00:54:50,290
So he gets taken to the church that serves as the burial ground for executed criminals.

813
00:54:50,650 --> 00:54:55,870
His funeral service is conducted not by the abbé who had been his confessor,

814
00:54:55,870 --> 00:55:00,250
but by a priest who is acceptable to the Republic, to the revolutionaries.

815
00:55:00,250 --> 00:55:04,010
His body is then thrown into a ditch on a bed of quicklime,

816
00:55:04,130 --> 00:55:05,910
which will help it become kind of mulch.

817
00:55:06,030 --> 00:55:09,830
The head is tossed in as well, and then it's covered by more quicklime.

818
00:55:10,250 --> 00:55:12,550
So this is a communal grave.

819
00:55:12,830 --> 00:55:14,570
There is to be no marker.

820
00:55:14,990 --> 00:55:19,830
Instead, it is left mingled up with other corpses where it will decompose.

821
00:55:19,890 --> 00:55:23,410
The whole thing, it's no special treatment whatsoever.

822
00:55:24,090 --> 00:55:25,450
And that's the intention.

823
00:55:25,810 --> 00:55:27,310
But of course, in the long run,

824
00:55:28,110 --> 00:55:28,970
it turns out,

825
00:55:28,970 --> 00:55:34,270
neither side really content with this spin on the execution of Louis or the fate of his body.

826
00:55:35,250 --> 00:55:38,850
And so for royalists, obviously, this is a terrible crime.

827
00:55:39,610 --> 00:55:42,630
And as had happened with Charles I, so with Louis XVI,

828
00:55:42,690 --> 00:55:47,350
it doesn't take them long before they are comparing Louis' fate to that of Christ.

829
00:55:47,730 --> 00:55:49,050
Right. A martyr.

830
00:55:49,270 --> 00:55:51,730
He's a martyr. Yeah, he's a Christian martyr.

831
00:55:51,910 --> 00:55:57,790
A martyr for monarchy, but above all, a martyr for the true church.

832
00:55:58,050 --> 00:55:58,290
Yeah.

833
00:55:58,970 --> 00:55:59,790
Republicans as well.

834
00:55:59,790 --> 00:56:02,670
And it may be that they are reacting to the kind of the royalist propaganda.

835
00:56:03,050 --> 00:56:10,030
They find it impossible to rest content with the idea that Louis had died simply as a common criminal.

836
00:56:10,410 --> 00:56:12,890
So already within a week of Louis' execution,

837
00:56:13,130 --> 00:56:16,510
you get newspapers claiming that his death, rather than being what it had been,

838
00:56:16,610 --> 00:56:18,250
which was something wholly unexceptional,

839
00:56:18,290 --> 00:56:22,110
that had been the whole point, had actually been something shameful and disgraceful.

840
00:56:22,310 --> 00:56:24,510
So in one paper, you get an account,

841
00:56:24,590 --> 00:56:28,450
at the instant that speech was so brutally taken from him by a definite beating of drums,

842
00:56:28,450 --> 00:56:30,330
Louis entered into a violent anger,

843
00:56:30,410 --> 00:56:33,070
stamped his foot, struggled with vigour with the executioner's assistance,

844
00:56:33,390 --> 00:56:38,070
let out terrible cries and fought to the end to such an extent that he had not his neck,

845
00:56:38,170 --> 00:56:42,770
but the back of his head and his jaw horribly mutilated by the fatal knife.

846
00:56:43,110 --> 00:56:46,550
So this is a claim that is quite often made,

847
00:56:46,670 --> 00:56:50,310
that it took two goes of the guillotine to sever his neck.

848
00:56:50,450 --> 00:56:51,030
Which is not true.

849
00:56:51,190 --> 00:56:53,530
And also there are reports that as he died,

850
00:56:53,570 --> 00:56:55,470
he let out a piercing scream.

851
00:56:55,590 --> 00:56:57,030
Again, not true.

852
00:56:57,030 --> 00:56:58,850
You know, his vocal cords have been severed.

853
00:56:58,930 --> 00:57:02,170
And there are also claims which supposedly derive from Saint-Saëns,

854
00:57:02,230 --> 00:57:04,950
and Saint-Saëns, you know, they become so repeated

855
00:57:04,950 --> 00:57:07,310
that Saint-Saëns actually has to publicly reject them,

856
00:57:07,390 --> 00:57:09,190
that on the night before his execution,

857
00:57:09,390 --> 00:57:13,770
and the hours before he's taken to the guillotine,

858
00:57:14,030 --> 00:57:15,930
Louis had been stuffing his face.

859
00:57:16,170 --> 00:57:19,910
And this is to repeat the old child, you know, that he's the pig king.

860
00:57:20,230 --> 00:57:23,190
And there are accounts that, you know,

861
00:57:23,190 --> 00:57:26,710
he'd been so fat that he hadn't fitted onto the board,

862
00:57:27,410 --> 00:57:29,270
that, you know, that he's executed on.

863
00:57:29,370 --> 00:57:30,590
And there's another one that he's, you know,

864
00:57:30,650 --> 00:57:34,210
the bulges and folds of his neck had been so obese

865
00:57:34,210 --> 00:57:35,970
that it wouldn't fit in the slot.

866
00:57:36,150 --> 00:57:37,130
Again, not true.

867
00:57:37,190 --> 00:57:38,330
I mean, simply not true.

868
00:57:38,550 --> 00:57:40,130
Yeah, because of course he'd been fasting,

869
00:57:40,210 --> 00:57:41,990
because for religious reasons.

870
00:57:42,190 --> 00:57:43,190
Yes, he had.

871
00:57:43,350 --> 00:57:45,250
And so it's an attempt to kind of contradict that.

872
00:57:45,450 --> 00:57:47,950
And what it does, of course,

873
00:57:48,610 --> 00:57:51,970
is to ensure that Louis' execution,

874
00:57:52,430 --> 00:57:54,210
rather than providing, as it were,

875
00:57:54,310 --> 00:57:56,690
a clear cut between the two,

876
00:57:56,710 --> 00:57:58,630
between the age of monarchy and the republic,

877
00:57:59,410 --> 00:58:02,550
and that the republic is now uncontested.

878
00:58:02,710 --> 00:58:04,270
It's not uncontested at all.

879
00:58:04,410 --> 00:58:08,690
The way in which both royalists and republicans

880
00:58:08,690 --> 00:58:12,310
try and rewrite the story of Louis' execution

881
00:58:12,310 --> 00:58:15,250
actually bodes very ill for France,

882
00:58:15,410 --> 00:58:18,670
because it suggests that there are deep,

883
00:58:18,830 --> 00:58:23,270
entrenched, principled opinions about what has been done,

884
00:58:23,410 --> 00:58:25,990
that there isn't really any hope

885
00:58:25,990 --> 00:58:27,730
of reconciling them.

886
00:58:28,230 --> 00:58:29,790
And it suggests that,

887
00:58:30,330 --> 00:58:34,710
even though perhaps the foreign threats to France

888
00:58:34,710 --> 00:58:36,110
have been beaten off,

889
00:58:36,250 --> 00:58:37,670
although who knows for how long,

890
00:58:37,670 --> 00:58:38,950
now that the king has been executed,

891
00:58:39,110 --> 00:58:41,310
who knows what the international response to that will be,

892
00:58:41,370 --> 00:58:42,750
but that may not be the worst of it,

893
00:58:42,830 --> 00:58:44,070
because it suggests that actually,

894
00:58:44,150 --> 00:58:45,930
within the body of France itself,

895
00:58:46,230 --> 00:58:48,490
a civil war may be brewing.

896
00:58:48,830 --> 00:58:49,150
Wow.

897
00:58:49,630 --> 00:58:54,750
What a dramatic end to the third series

898
00:58:54,750 --> 00:58:56,490
on the French Revolution.

899
